<h2>The short answer</h2>
<p>Point the panel at the equator and tilt it to about your latitude. At 35° N, that means a 35° tilt from horizontal, facing true south. In Sydney (about 34° S), it means roughly 34°, facing true north.</p>
<p>For a security camera, winter is the season that matters, because that is when days are short, the sun is low and the battery is most likely to run flat. So for most owners, the best angle for solar security camera panel setups is <strong>latitude + 15°</strong>, set once and never touched again. Summer output will still be more than the camera needs.</p>
<p>Enter your location below to get a tilt and direction for your exact latitude, or keep reading for the reasoning, a city table and the mistakes that cost far more than a few degrees.</p>

<!--tool:panel-angle-->

<h2>Why the solar camera panel angle matters</h2>
<p>A solar panel produces the most power when sunlight hits it square-on. As the angle between the sun’s rays and the panel’s surface moves away from 90°, the panel intercepts less light. The loss follows the cosine of the error angle, which is why small errors barely matter and large ones add up quickly.</p>
<p>Camera panels are small, typically 3–6 W, with some kits at 10–20 W. In real conditions they often deliver only 60–80% of their rating after heat, dirt and charging losses. A 5 W panel in December in the northern US might see 2–3 peak sun hours, which works out to roughly 6–12 Wh per day. That is close to what a busy camera uses. A bad angle or a bit of shade is often the difference between a battery that holds steady and one that slowly drains. Our guide to <a href="/how-solar-cameras-work/">how solar cameras work</a> explains the full energy chain from panel to battery.</p>

<div class="keyfacts">
<div><b>≈ latitude</b><span>best fixed year-round tilt</span></div>
<div><b>+15°</b><span>steeper for winter priority</span></div>
<div><b>True south</b><span>panel direction north of the equator</span></div>
<div><b>~2–4%</b><span>loss from a 10–15° tilt error</span></div>
<div><b>15–25%</b><span>typical loss facing east or west</span></div>
<div><b>50%+</b><span>possible loss from partial shade</span></div>
</div>

<h2>The latitude rule: solar panel tilt for a camera by latitude</h2>
<p>The sun’s height at midday depends on how far you are from the equator and the time of year. At the equinoxes in March and September, the midday sun sits at 90° minus your latitude above the horizon. A panel tilted at your latitude faces it directly.</p>
<p>Through the year, the sun’s midday height swings about 23.5° above and below that equinox position. The common rule of thumb adjusts by 15° rather than the full 23.5°, because it averages over the whole season rather than one day:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Year-round:</strong> tilt = latitude.</li>
<li><strong>Winter priority:</strong> tilt = latitude + 15°.</li>
<li><strong>Summer priority:</strong> tilt = latitude − 15°.</li>
</ul>
<p>Tilt is measured from horizontal. A panel lying flat is 0°; a panel bolted flat against a wall is 90°.</p>

<h3>What happens if the panel is too flat or too steep</h3>
<p>A panel that is <strong>too flat</strong> catches high summer sun well but misses the low winter sun, which is exactly when your camera needs the energy. Flat panels also collect dust, pollen, leaves and snow, and rain does not rinse them as well.</p>
<p>A panel that is <strong>too steep</strong> loses some summer output, but summer usually has energy to spare. The steeper angle helps snow slide off, lets rain wash the surface and lines up better with the winter sun. For a battery camera, erring steep is almost always the safer mistake.</p>

<div class="callout tip"><strong>Rule of thumb for cameras</strong><p>If you will only set the panel once, use latitude + 10° to + 15°. You give up a little summer output that you would not have used anyway, and you gain the winter margin that keeps the camera online.</p></div>

