<p>Two practical notes. First, a minimum tilt of around 10–15° is worth keeping even in the tropics so rain can rinse the panel. Second, at high latitudes such as Anchorage or Edinburgh, winter sun is so weak and brief that no tilt fully solves it; plan for a larger panel or extra battery, as covered in our guide to <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-work-in-winter/">whether solar cameras work in winter</a>.</p>

<h2>Which direction should a solar camera panel face?</h2>
<p>In the northern hemisphere, face the panel <strong>true south</strong>. In the southern hemisphere, face it <strong>true north</strong>. Either way, you are pointing it toward the equator, where the sun spends the middle of the day.</p>
<p>The camera and the panel do not need to point the same way. Most kits connect the panel with a 3–4 m cable, so you can aim the camera at your driveway and mount the panel on a south-facing gutter, fence or wall. Extension cables are available for many models when the best sun is farther away. See our <a href="/solar-camera-installation/">solar camera installation guide</a> for mounting positions.</p>

<h3>True south vs magnetic south: magnetic declination</h3>
<p>A compass points to magnetic north, not the geographic North Pole. The difference is called <strong>magnetic declination</strong>, and it varies by location and slowly changes over time. In parts of the US it is near zero; in others it is 15° or more.</p>
<p>As rough examples from the mid-2020s, Seattle sits around 15° east and Boston around 14° west, while much of the central US is close to zero. Sydney is roughly 12–13° east. Always look up your own value.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Find your declination</strong><p>Enter your ZIP code or coordinates into the <a href="https://www.ngdc.noaa.gov/geomag/calculators/magcalc.shtml" rel="noopener" target="_blank">NOAA magnetic declination calculator</a>. It returns a value such as 15° E or 14° W.</p></li>
<li><strong>Convert to a compass bearing</strong><p>True south is 180°. Subtract an east declination or add a west one. With 15° E, aim the panel at 165° on the compass. With 14° W, aim it at 194°.</p></li>
<li><strong>Or use a phone set to true north</strong><p>Many phone compass apps have a “true north” setting that applies declination automatically using your location. Turn it on, then aim at 180° (or 0° in the southern hemisphere).</p></li>
<li><strong>Check against the sun</strong><p>At solar noon the sun is due south (or due north south of the equator). The <a href="https://gml.noaa.gov/grad/solcalc/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">NOAA solar calculator</a> gives the exact solar noon time for your location, which is often not 12:00 on the clock.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>If your declination is under about 5°, you can ignore it. A 5° direction error costs well under 1% of output.</p>

<h2>East or west when south is shaded</h2>
<p>Many homes simply do not have a clear south-facing spot near the camera. The usual compromise is east or west. At typical camera-panel tilts, an east- or west-facing panel generally produces around 15–25% less energy over a year than a south-facing one, with larger losses in winter at higher latitudes.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Southeast or southwest</strong> is much better than due east or west. A panel 45° off south loses only a small share of output.</li>
<li><strong>West</strong> catches warmer afternoon sun, which is often clearer in places with morning fog or haze.</li>
<li><strong>East</strong> starts charging early and dries dew and frost sooner, but cold mornings can delay charging if the battery is below its charge temperature limit.</li>
<li><strong>Flatter tilts</strong> help east- and west-facing panels, because a flatter panel catches more of the sky as the sun crosses it. Around 15–25° often works better than a steep angle when facing east or west.</li>
<li><strong>North-facing</strong> (in the northern hemisphere) should be a last resort. In winter it may produce almost nothing.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are trying to decide whether a given spot is good enough, our <a href="/tools/solar-camera-site-check/">solar camera site check</a> walks you through the questions in a minute.</p>

<h2>How much does the wrong angle actually cost?</h2>
<p>Less than most people expect. If you cannot hit the best angle for solar security camera panel mounting exactly, do not worry. Because the loss follows the cosine of the error, small mistakes are nearly free. The figures below show the instantaneous loss when the sun is off-axis by a given amount; over a full day, the real loss is usually smaller still.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Angle error</th><th>Share of light intercepted</th><th>Approximate loss</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>5°</td><td>99.6%</td><td>&lt;1%</td></tr>
<tr><td>10°</td><td>98.5%</td><td>~1.5%</td></tr>
<tr><td>15°</td><td>96.6%</td><td>~3.4%</td></tr>
<tr><td>25°</td><td>90.6%</td><td>~9%</td></tr>
<tr><td>45°</td><td>70.7%</td><td>~29%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

<p>Compare that with shade. Small camera panels are made of solar cells wired in series, so shading part of the panel can drag down the whole string. A branch shadow across a few cells, a strip of shade from a gutter edge or a layer of grime can easily cost more than half the output. Our guide to <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-need-direct-sunlight/">whether solar cameras need direct sunlight</a> covers how much power panels make in shade and cloud.</p>

<blockquote>Get the shade right first, the direction second and the tilt third. A perfectly angled panel in shade loses to a roughly angled panel in full sun every time.</blockquote>

<h2>Shade and obstruction checks before you drill</h2>
<p>The sun takes a very different path in December than in June. A spot that gets eight hours of sun in summer can sit in a building’s shadow all winter, because the low winter sun is blocked by things that never cast a shadow in summer.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Look south at the winter sun height</strong><p>At midday on the winter solstice, the sun sits at about 90° minus your latitude minus 23.5° above the horizon. At 40° N that is only about 26.5°. Anything that rises more than that angle above the panel, such as a house, fence or evergreen tree, will cast a shadow at noon in December.</p></li>
<li><strong>Check morning and afternoon too</strong><p>In winter the sun rises in the southeast and sets in the southwest. Obstructions on either side of south shorten the charging window.</p></li>
<li><strong>Remember deciduous trees</strong><p>Bare branches still block a meaningful share of light, and a tree that is bare in winter will shade the panel fully in summer.</p></li>
<li><strong>Watch your own eaves</strong><p>A panel tucked tight under a roof overhang can be shaded for much of the summer day when the sun is high.</p></li>
<li><strong>Take photos at 10 a.m., noon and 2 p.m.</strong><p>A quick set of photos from the panel position on a sunny day shows you exactly where shadows fall. Repeat in late fall if you can.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>For a numbers-based check of expected output at your address, <a href="https://pvwatts.nrel.gov/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">NREL’s PVWatts calculator</a> estimates monthly solar energy for any tilt and direction. It is designed for rooftop systems, but the monthly patterns apply to a camera panel too.</p>

<h2>Camera-integrated panels vs separate panels</h2>
<p>Some cameras have a solar panel built into the top of the housing. Others use a separate panel on its own bracket. The angle advice applies to both, but your options differ.</p>
<div class="proscons">
<div class="pros"><h4>Built-in panel</h4><ul><li>One device, one mount, no cable to route.</li><li>Fewer connectors to fail or let water in.</li><li>Tidy appearance.</li></ul></div>
<div class="cons"><h4>Built-in panel limitations</h4><ul><li>The panel points wherever the camera points, which is rarely south.</li><li>Often nearly flat, so it collects dirt and snow.</li><li>Under eaves, it may get little direct sun.</li><li>Usually smaller than a separate panel.</li></ul></div>
</div>
<p>Separate panels win when the camera must face north or sit under an overhang. If your camera has a built-in panel and struggles, check whether the maker sells an add-on panel. Our <a href="/best-solar-security-cameras/">best solar security cameras</a> guide covers which features to look for.</p>

<h2>Mounting hardware and angle adjustment</h2>
<p>Most camera panel kits include a bracket with a ball joint or a hinged arm. These are convenient but are rarely marked in degrees, so you have to measure.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Measure tilt with a phone.</strong> Lay the phone flat on the panel and open the level or measure app. It shows the angle from horizontal. Many free inclinometer apps do the same.</li>
<li><strong>Tighten firmly.</strong> Ball joints can slowly sag under wind and snow load. Recheck the angle after the first storm.</li>
<li><strong>Use a pole mount</strong> for fences and posts. Pole brackets usually allow a wider tilt range than wall brackets.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid drilling if needed.</strong> Gutter clips and clamp mounts can hold a small panel at a good angle; see <a href="/how-to/install-without-drilling/">how to install a solar camera without drilling</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the cable drip loop.</strong> Let the cable dip below the connector before it rises, so water runs off instead of into the plug.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Winter solar panel angle and seasonal adjustment schedule</h2>
<p>Adjusting the tilt through the year can add some energy, but for a camera panel it is usually not worth climbing a ladder four times a year. If you do want to adjust, keep it simple.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>When to change (N hemisphere)</th><th>Tilt setting</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Set and forget</td><td>Never</td><td>Latitude + 10° to + 15°</td><td>Most homes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Two seasons</td><td>Mid-October and mid-March</td><td>Latitude + 15° (winter), latitude − 15° (summer)</td><td>Busy cameras in northern areas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Four seasons</td><td>Around each equinox and solstice</td><td>Lat + 15°, lat, lat − 15°, lat</td><td>Off-grid sites with larger panels</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>South of the equator, shift the dates by six months: steepen in mid-April, flatten in mid-September. Where snow is common, a winter tilt of 50–60° or steeper also helps snow slide off within a day or two of a storm.</p>

<!--products:panel-reolink,panel-usbc,ext-cable,pole-mount-->

<h2>Troubleshooting low charge after install</h2>
<p>If the battery level still drops after you have set the best angle for solar security camera panel performance, work through these causes in order. Most are not angle problems at all.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Confirm the panel is actually charging</strong><p>Open the camera’s app and check its charge status in daylight. If it never shows charging, see our <a href="/troubleshooting/not-charging/">solar camera not charging</a> guide.</p></li>
<li><strong>Look for shade at different times</strong><p>Check the panel at mid-morning and mid-afternoon, not just at noon.</p></li>
<li><strong>Clean the panel</strong><p>A film of dust or pollen, bird droppings or hard-water spots can cut output noticeably. Wipe with water and a soft cloth.</p></li>
<li><strong>Check for cold</strong><p>Many cameras will not charge their lithium battery below about 0 °C (32 °F), so frosty mornings produce no charge even in sun.</p></li>
<li><strong>Measure your consumption</strong><p>Busy scenes, long clips and lots of live viewing can use more energy than a small panel can supply. The <a href="/tools/solar-camera-battery-calculator/">solar camera battery calculator</a> shows your daily budget.</p></li>
<li><strong>Reduce triggers</strong><p>Narrow the motion zones, lower sensitivity and shorten clip length. These changes often save more energy than any angle tweak can add.</p></li>
</ol>
<div class="callout info"><strong>Still short on power?</strong><p>If the panel is unshaded, clean and well angled, and the camera still drains, you need more panel, not a better angle. A second panel or a larger one is the real fix. Our guide on <a href="/guides/solar-camera-battery-life-no-sun/">how long solar cameras last without sun</a> explains how to size the battery buffer.</p></div>
